<div class="kg-note ice"><div class="title">Read the season as a pipeline, not 720 isolated moves</div><div class="body">Early turns create capacity, middle turns compound it, and late turns protect realizable value. The useful question is not “What action pays most now?” but “Which action improves the amount of cash that can still reach the bank before turn 720?”</div></div>","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.007148,"end_time":"2026-08-08T16:25:13.868865+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-08T16:25:13.861717+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]}},{"id":"ae33880c","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 6. Four practical control layers

<div class="kg-grid">
  <div class="kg-card indigo"><div class="eyebrow">A · Complete season route</div><b>Let the long plan do the heavy lifting.</b><br><span>A compact full-season trajectory coordinates labor, crops, livestock, logistics, and planned market activity across the 720-turn episode.</span></div>
  <div class="kg-card ember"><div class="eyebrow">B · Actor-local repair</div><b>Fix a blocked action without inventing a new strategy.</b><br><span>If a WEED blocks an intended PLANT or BUILD_PASTURE action, that actor digs, retries the intended work, and briefly replays its route before rejoining.</span></div>
  <div class="kg-card crimson"><div class="eyebrow">C · Demand-aware SELL ordering</div><b>Protect the most fragile queue position first.</b><br><span>Existing SELL orders are ranked by modeled quote damage, with a small urgency adjustment from the live town demand. Duplicate shop instances are counted independently.</span></div>
  <div class="kg-card ice"><div class="eyebrow">D · Multi-horizon clone preemption</div><b>Reach a predictable premium sale before a near clone does.</b><br><span>When public farm states are almost identical, the controller prefers a safe sale three turns ahead; if that shift is unavailable, it falls back to two turns and then one. The moved quantity is subtracted exactly when the original sale becomes due.</span></div>
</div>

<div class="kg-prose"><div class="lead">The market layer has two jobs. First, it ranks existing SELL slots by nonlinear price damage and observed demand. Second, only under a tight near-clone gate, it changes <b>timing</b>: move future premium quantity earlier, record the amount, and repay it later. At the $1 price floor the preemption is skipped because that sale cannot add further supply pressure.</div></div>

<div class="kg-note ice"><div class="title">Why three, then two, then one?</div><div class="body">A public route can make both one-turn and two-turn responses predictable. The controller therefore checks three turns first, then falls back to two and one. The longer horizon is still tightly gated by public-state similarity, inventory availability, price floor, and exact quantity repayment.</div></div>


<div class="kg-takeaway">
  <div class="label">Takeaway · Section 6</div>
  <ul>
    <li><b>A</b> carries the economics, <b>B</b> keeps execution physically possible, <b>C</b> defends queue position, <b>D</b> defends timing against a near-clone.</li>
    <li><b>Each layer is separately falsifiable.</b> Disable one and the season still runs — which is exactly what makes their contributions measurable.</li>
    <li><b>Layer D repays what it borrows.</b> Shifting a premium sale earlier removes the same quantity when it came due, so timing adaptation never quietly becomes a production change.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="verdict"><b>So what:</b> layers that can be switched off one at a time can also be <i>attributed</i> one at a time. Design for the ablation you will want later.</div>
</div>
